1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,400
This object was found
inside a layer of rock.

2
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:07,580
It's a rectangle of glass and
metal, with a dead battery inside.

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:11,440
The Ancients carried one of these
in their pocket all day long.

4
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:13,740
They slept with it right beside them.

5
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:15,480
We still don't quite know why.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,740
Welcome to the Museum of
the Age of the Ancients.

7
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:20,320
I'm Lume, the curator.

8
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:23,720
And today we're opening
the first display case:

9
00:00:23,940 --> 00:00:25,600
what was left of them.

10
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:30,020
The Ancients lived between the
twentieth and twenty-first centuries.

11
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:31,960
There were eight billion of them.

12
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,040
And they made things like
no species before them.

13
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:39,420
Around twenty twenty, the weight of
everything they had built, buildings,

14
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:44,040
roads, machines, became equal to the
weight of all living things on the

15
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:45,120
planet combined.

16
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:48,180
And yet, we know very little about them.

17
00:00:48,580 --> 00:00:51,380
The Ancients kept almost
everything in digital form:

18
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:54,440
photos, letters, books, music.

19
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:56,580
And digital files age badly.

20
00:00:57,040 --> 00:01:01,340
Every time they changed machines, a
little more became impossible to open.

21
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:06,020
In twenty fifteen, one of the creators
of the internet, Vint Cerf, warned

22
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:09,020
that their era could
become a digital dark age.

23
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:12,760
That's why, here at the museum, we
study the Ancients the way you study

24
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:15,300
ruins: by what's left on the ground.

25
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:17,320
So let's run an experiment.

26
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:22,560
Imagine the Ancients left the stage and
no one stayed behind to fix anything.

27
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:24,640
Let's move the clock forward.

28
00:01:25,080 --> 00:01:25,960
A hundred years.

29
00:01:26,380 --> 00:01:27,320
A thousand years.

30
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:28,880
Ten thousand years.

31
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:31,500
Some things start changing right away.

32
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:36,020
Beneath New York, the subway pumps
remove about thirteen million gallons

33
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:39,160
of water every day that seep
in through the tunnel walls.

34
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:42,460
With no one to run the pumps,
the tunnels begin to flood.

35
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:46,530
In the first hundred years, what
seems strongest falls first:

36
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:48,420
reinforced concrete.

37
00:01:48,940 --> 00:01:50,800
Inside it there are steel bars.

38
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:55,300
When water gets in through a crack,
the steel rusts, and rust takes up more

39
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:56,720
space than the steel did.

40
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:00,480
It pushes the concrete from the
inside out, until it breaks.

41
00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:05,380
Bridges, overpasses and
skyscrapers give way, one by one.

42
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:11,560
Meanwhile, Roman concrete buildings,
with no steel inside, have been standing

43
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:13,700
for about two thousand years.

44
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:19,120
After a thousand years, the cities
have become hills covered in brush.

45
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:23,540
The Ancients did try to protect
themselves from being forgotten.

46
00:02:24,020 --> 00:02:29,380
In an old coal mine in the Arctic, in
Svalbard, they stored data on photographic

47
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:32,540
film, designed to last
about a thousand years.

48
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:37,600
But what survives best of a civilization
is almost never what it wanted

49
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:40,380
to keep. It's what it threw away.

50
00:02:41,140 --> 00:02:45,100
And the Ancients threw things
away in a very particular way:

51
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:50,500
in landfills, mountains of
compacted trash covered with dirt.

52
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:55,460
In the late nineteen eighties, an
archaeologist from the Ancients themselves,

53
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:01,120
William Rathje, of the University of
Arizona, decided to dig into these

54
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:03,240
landfills as if they were ruins.

55
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:07,400
He found newspapers from decades
earlier that could still be read.

56
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:12,540
And hot dogs that had been buried for
about fifteen years that still looked

57
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:13,580
like hot dogs.

58
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:17,740
In there it's so dry and packed
that almost nothing rots.

59
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:21,420
Archaeologists have known
this for a long time.

60
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:26,660
The most common find at many
digs isn't gold or statues.

61
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:28,340
It's broken pottery.

62
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:32,720
Fired clay doesn't rot like wood
and doesn't rust like metal.

63
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:35,280
And nobody reuses a broken pot:

64
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:37,480
it goes straight into the trash.

65
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:42,680
So, ten thousand years from now, the
Ancients will be remembered by shards

66
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:45,520
of mugs, of tiles and of toilets.

67
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:48,180
Lots and lots of toilets.

68
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:53,060
After ten thousand years, some
things have barely changed.

69
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:57,740
At Mount Rushmore, in the United
States, the Ancients carved the faces of

70
00:03:57,760 --> 00:03:59,560
four presidents into granite.

71
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:04,650
By the park geologists' estimate, the
rock loses only about one inch every

72
00:04:04,670 --> 00:04:06,120
ten thousand years.

73
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:12,300
The four faces will still be there,
serious, looking out over a forest with

74
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:13,440
no one in it.

75
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:16,480
And their trash is still there too.

76
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:22,120
By twenty fifteen alone, they had made
more than eight billion tons of plastic.

77
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:26,980
Of the part that became waste,
seventy-nine percent ended up in landfills

78
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,360
or in nature.

79
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:30,520
Plastic doesn't rot like wood.

80
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:34,580
It just breaks into
smaller and smaller pieces.

81
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,800
And sometimes, it turns into stone.

82
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:43,280
In twenty fourteen, on a beach in
Hawaii, geologists described a new kind

83
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:48,960
of rock: plastic melted in campfires,
mixed with sand, shells and bits of

84
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:51,340
lava. They named it plastiglomerate.

85
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:54,200
It's heavier than plastic alone.

86
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:58,220
It sinks, gets buried and
can enter the rock record.

87
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:03,060
Now, here's a detail that
always surprises our visitors.

88
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:07,360
The most common fossil of the
Ancients isn't a human bone.

89
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:08,840
It's a chicken bone.

90
00:05:09,360 --> 00:05:13,360
In twenty eighteen, British researchers
showed that the chicken raised by

91
00:05:13,380 --> 00:05:18,100
the Ancients was a new animal: at least
twice the size of a medieval chicken,

92
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:22,960
with a skeleton, bone chemistry and
genetics unlike any of its ancestors.

93
00:05:23,460 --> 00:05:27,800
And there were about twenty-three
billion of them alive at the same time.

94
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:32,440
To a paleontologist of the future,
this may very well be the age of the

95
00:05:32,460 --> 00:05:33,020
chicken.

96
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:36,880
But there's one mark no
future scientist will miss.

97
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:41,160
Starting in the nineteen fifties, atomic
bomb tests spread plutonium across

98
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:42,680
the entire planet.

99
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:47,040
It fell with the rain and settled at
the bottom of lakes and seas, in a

100
00:05:47,060 --> 00:05:49,520
thin layer, all over the world.

101
00:05:50,220 --> 00:05:54,300
Plutonium two thirty-nine takes about
twenty-four thousand years to lose

102
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:55,790
half its radioactivity.

103
00:05:55,810 --> 00:06:00,500
Ten thousand years later, the layer
is still there, easy to detect.

104
00:06:00,880 --> 00:06:05,240
At a lake in Canada, Crawford Lake,
the Ancients' geologists even proposed

105
00:06:05,260 --> 00:06:08,380
this layer as the start of
a new epoch of the Earth:

106
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:09,700
the Anthropocene.

107
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:13,320
In twenty twenty-four,
the proposal was rejected.

108
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:15,680
The Ancients couldn't even agree on that.

109
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:18,760
And there's an even subtler mark:

110
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:24,940
the air. In Antarctica, every layer of
snow that turned into ice traps tiny

111
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:27,340
bubbles of the air of its time.

112
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:31,960
Before factories, the air held about
two hundred eighty parts per million

113
00:06:31,980 --> 00:06:33,220
of carbon dioxide.

114
00:06:33,740 --> 00:06:37,820
In May twenty twenty-five, the peak
went past four hundred thirty.

115
00:06:38,380 --> 00:06:42,320
Whoever drills into the ice from this
era will find, trapped in bubbles,

116
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:45,500
the breath of a civilization in a hurry.

117
00:06:45,740 --> 00:06:49,660
And there's one message the Ancients
wrote to the future on purpose.

118
00:06:50,130 --> 00:06:54,020
They buried their most dangerous nuclear
waste in tunnels hundreds of meters

119
00:06:54,040 --> 00:06:59,660
deep. In Finland, the first repository
of its kind, called Onkalo, received

120
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:02,460
a license to operate in
October twenty twenty-six.

121
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:07,860
But how do you warn someone, thousands
of years from now, not to dig there?

122
00:07:08,380 --> 00:07:10,260
No language lasts that long.

123
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:15,060
In the United States, a report from
nineteen ninety-three drafted the text

124
00:07:15,080 --> 00:07:16,900
of just such a warning.

125
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:21,740
It begins like this: This
place is not a place of honor.

126
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:23,760
Nothing valued is here.

127
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:27,140
It's strange and beautiful
at the same time:

128
00:07:27,660 --> 00:07:32,360
one of the Ancients' first letters to
the future was a request to stay away.

129
00:07:33,220 --> 00:07:34,800
Now let's go further.

130
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:36,600
One million years.

131
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:40,100
The cities have become a
layer of compressed rubble.

132
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:43,580
The plastic has become a dark
sheet between the rocks.

133
00:07:44,220 --> 00:07:49,740
In twenty eighteen, two scientists,
Gavin Schmidt, of NASA, and Adam Frank,

134
00:07:49,940 --> 00:07:51,780
asked an uncomfortable question:

135
00:07:52,200 --> 00:07:56,440
if an industrial civilization had
existed on Earth millions of years ago,

136
00:07:56,500 --> 00:07:58,020
would we be able to tell?

137
00:07:58,580 --> 00:08:01,360
Their answer: it wouldn't be easy.

138
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:06,260
Given enough time, an entire civilization
can become just a strange band

139
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:12,380
in the rock: a shift in carbon, traces
of plastic, rare elements, that from

140
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:16,480
far away looks like other
natural changes in Earth's past.

141
00:08:17,260 --> 00:08:18,860
Eight billion people.

142
00:08:19,400 --> 00:08:21,100
A century in a hurry.

143
00:08:21,460 --> 00:08:24,960
And in the end, a thin
band of different rock.

144
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:29,000
The clearest traces of the
Ancients will be far from here.

145
00:08:29,320 --> 00:08:33,560
On the Moon, where there's no wind or
rain, the astronauts' footprints could

146
00:08:33,580 --> 00:08:35,240
last millions of years.

147
00:08:35,640 --> 00:08:40,580
About twenty-two thousand miles up,
dead satellites, pushed into an orbit

148
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:44,460
called the graveyard, could keep
circling for millions of years.

149
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:49,680
Two probes, the Voyagers, carry a
golden record with sounds and images of

150
00:08:49,700 --> 00:08:53,640
Earth, made to last about a
billion years among the stars.

151
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:58,380
And the Ancients' radio broadcasts
already form a bubble around the Earth

152
00:08:58,400 --> 00:09:01,220
with a radius of more than
a hundred light-years.

153
00:09:01,560 --> 00:09:04,620
But there's one thing the
Ancients were slow to realize.

154
00:09:04,940 --> 00:09:08,240
The layer they would leave
behind hadn't been written yet.

155
00:09:08,500 --> 00:09:11,660
Every layer of rock is
just a record of choices.

156
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:13,880
More plastic or less.

157
00:09:14,340 --> 00:09:15,960
More carbon or less.

158
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:18,060
More bombs or less.

159
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:19,920
And they started to choose.

160
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:23,960
When they discovered that a gas from
refrigerators and spray cans was opening

161
00:09:23,980 --> 00:09:28,420
a hole in the ozone layer, they signed
an agreement, the Montreal Protocol,

162
00:09:28,440 --> 00:09:30,040
in nineteen eighty-seven.

163
00:09:30,340 --> 00:09:32,660
And the layer began to recover.

164
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:36,120
They recorded archives
built to last for centuries.

165
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:40,900
They buried the most dangerous waste
deep underground, with warnings for

166
00:09:40,920 --> 00:09:42,220
whoever came after.

167
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:46,220
And they started measuring what they
were leaving in the ground, precisely

168
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:47,560
so they could change it.

169
00:09:48,340 --> 00:09:53,000
The Ancients were brilliant and
completely lost at the same time.

170
00:09:53,140 --> 00:09:57,080
But they were the first civilization
that knew it was turning into rock.

171
00:09:57,860 --> 00:10:02,040
And that gave them a chance no
people before them ever had:

172
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:04,340
deciding what would be left.

173
00:10:05,060 --> 00:10:08,560
So, let's write the label
for object number one.

174
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:10,770
Rectangle of glass and metal.

175
00:10:10,790 --> 00:10:12,780
Twenty-first century.

176
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:15,960
The Ancients kept the whole world in here.

177
00:10:16,420 --> 00:10:17,880
Nothing was left in here.

178
00:10:18,060 --> 00:10:20,440
What was left is what they did out there.

179
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:25,960
In the next display case: why the
Ancients spent hours a day staring at

180
00:10:25,980 --> 00:10:27,420
glowing rectangles.

181
00:10:27,680 --> 00:10:28,920
What about you?

182
00:10:29,280 --> 00:10:32,340
What object from your home
should go into the museum?

183
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:33,700
Tell me in the comments.

184
00:10:33,960 --> 00:10:35,200
I read every one.
